#---
# purpose: "a non-tail recursion whose return type is a nesting ladder: {n, {n, ... :start}}"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# The program itself is ordinary and correct on every door. What it pins is the
# compiler's cost of inferring its return. `build/1` returns `:start` or a pair
# whose second field is another `build/1` return, so its recursive-return
# component names the return directly as a regular denotation,
# mu t.(:start | {integer, t}), on its own evidence -- no ladder of
# ever-deeper approximations, no widening.
# `compiler2_tuple_return_ladder_settles_via_component_solve` in the drive
# tests measures that cost.
def build(0), do: :start
def build(n), do: {n, build(n - 1)}

def main() do
  dbg(build(3))
end
